WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.980
The Human Genome Project took about 13 years to read human DNA.

2
00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:11.800
A genome is all the DNA in one set of chromosomes.

3
00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:21.000
By 2022, the estimated cost to read a human-sized genome was about 525 What changed?

4
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.480
Nobody can see DNA the way you see a pencil.

5
00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:28.020
Its shape was first worked out from evidence and models.

6
00:00:29.083 --> 00:00:33.163
A model is a simplified picture that helps explain something real.

7
00:00:34.243 --> 00:00:40.363
DNA has four bases: A, T, G and C. A flat ladder model shows which bases pair up,

8
00:00:40.683 --> 00:00:41.683
but leaves out the twist.

9
00:00:42.563 --> 00:00:45.603
A string of letters shows base order, but leaves out the shape.

10
00:00:46.483 --> 00:00:47.763
Every model hides something.

11
00:00:48.767 --> 00:00:52.767
By the early 1950s, scientists had three kinds of evidence.

12
00:00:53.707 --> 00:00:54.707
First, base amounts.

13
00:00:55.627 --> 00:01:01.067
In a DNA sample, the amount of A is close to the amount of T. G is close to C.

14
00:01:01.787 --> 00:01:03.187
Second, X-ray patterns.

15
00:01:04.027 --> 00:01:07.127
X-rays shone through DNA fibers make spots on film.

16
00:01:08.047 --> 00:01:12.047
Their spacing pointed to a helix, a coil, about 2 nm wide.

17
00:01:12.867 --> 00:01:15.307
It gave shape clues, not a photo of the bases.

18
00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:17.910
Third, model building.

19
00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:22.910
Watson and Crick fitted these facts together with the known chemistry of the parts.

20
00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:28.730
In 1953, they published the double helix: two strands coiled around each other,

21
00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:31.150
backbones outside, bases inside.

22
00:01:32.190 --> 00:01:37.030
Base pairing is the rule that A pairs with T, and G pairs with C. They called the

23
00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:40.090
model unproved. Later tests supported it.

24
00:01:40.933 --> 00:01:43.533
Knowing the shape did not give the order of the bases.

25
00:01:44.893 --> 00:01:47.733
DNA sequencing is any method that reads that order.

26
00:01:48.633 --> 00:01:52.293
In 1977, Frederick Sanger and colleagues published one.

27
00:01:53.033 --> 00:01:55.613
It stops copies of a strand at each kind of base.

28
00:01:56.493 --> 00:01:58.513
The lengths of the copies reveal the order.

29
00:01:59.393 --> 00:02:05.073
From 1990 to 2003, the Human Genome Project mostly used improved versions.

30
00:02:05.973 --> 00:02:09.033
It made a reference sequence: a standard to compare against.

31
00:02:09.033 --> 00:02:10.033
www .mooji .org

32
00:02:10.067 --> 00:02:14.787
So what changed? The main features of the double helix model still hold.

33
00:02:15.707 --> 00:02:18.727
Sequencing added the letters, and how they differ between people.

34
00:02:19.667 --> 00:02:24.247
Now that reading DNA costs far less, biologists can compare many people and species.

35
00:02:25.267 --> 00:02:28.507
Keep this rule: every model shows some things and hides others.
